license: CC-BY-4.0
keywords:
  - de Sitter space
  - Wormholes
  - Kaluza–Klein gravitons
  - Golden ratio
  - Randall–Sundrum model
  - Cosmological constant
  - Formal verification
  - Lean 4
  - Mathlib4
abstract: "In de Sitter space an observer must be inside the universe it
  observes, and in string theory placing it has become a choice: of a vacuum
  among some 10^500. The same choice returns where wormholes, black holes and
  baby universes meet. The baby universes that branch off through wormholes
  carry parameters that fix the constants of nature, the cosmological constant
  among them, and the same parameters label the boundary theories over which the
  gravity of a black-hole throat is averaged; in both the question is which
  value, and the answer is sought in what a throat contains. The programme to
  which this note belongs, written within critical realism, reads the difficulty
  as one of representation rather than of selection: what is missing is one
  object that meets the criteria Witten and Chandrasekaran, Longo, Penington and
  Witten set for an observer in de Sitter, not a choice among values. This note
  does not test that claim itself; it tests two of its consequences where data
  can reach them, on the particles of one throat and on the cosmological
  constant. The object, in the framework of the programme, is a microstate whose
  levels form a tower built from two numbers, π and the golden ratio φ; read as
  a warped extra dimension it is a throat, the golden throat, whose Kaluza–Klein
  modes are its particles. The question becomes whether the tower fixes what a
  throat model otherwise chooses. The two-brane model of Randall and Sundrum
  assumes the dimension and chooses the infrared scale and the depth. The golden
  throat has the same equations, so the same mass ratios, and four independent
  conditions of the tower, reflection positivity, four-dimensional gravity, the
  arity of the framework and the clock of φ, fix its spectrum with no free
  parameter, to leading order π(k+1/4) times Mφ^12 in the unit M of the
  framework. The arity gives the dimension that the two-brane model assumes;
  with M identified with the scale of the charged leptons and the level 12 with
  the bottom of the throat, the spectrum fixes the infrared scale that it
  chooses. Identified with the graviton candidates reported at 380 and 700 GeV,
  they place the bottom of the throat at 11.97±0.05, 0.6 standard deviations
  from 12, and give the next modes at 1028 and 1346 GeV, against 1014 and 1328
  GeV for the two-brane model fitted to the same candidates. Only the depth, the
  position of the top, stays free, and the ratio of the vector modes can decide
  it if they share the throat. The same tower carries the observer of de Sitter
  space. In the gravitational unit of the framework the observed cosmological
  constant gives the observer's level, 583.79±0.03: the level is read from the
  data, not chosen, and the hypothesis that it sits at an integer level is
  excluded within ΛCDM at 7.6 standard deviations. What the note leaves open is
  one number, the ratio of the lepton scale to the Planck scale."
